winemac: while a program holds the mouse (mouse look), send its input as it arrives and drop none.

While a program hides and confines the cursor, Cocoa mouse events were posted to the event queue of
the window's thread, so they reached the server only when that thread pumped messages: once a frame,
in one lump. SDL 3 reads raw input on a thread of its own (GetRawInputBuffer in relative mode), so
each lump raced the game's read and its motion landed in that frame or the next. The camera turned
by none, one or two frames' worth of motion while everything else moved smoothly. Motion was also
lost outright: every click, release, scroll or warp forced the next move to be absolute, an absolute
move replaced the relative motion still queued, and a confined cursor's absolute move is a raw delta
of zero.

While the mouse is locked (cursor hidden and clipped):
  * the first ClipCursor starts a time-critical mouse thread with its own event queue, and moves,
    buttons and scroll go to that queue, so they are sent the moment they arrive;
  * moves are sent as relative deltas only; a forced absolute move waits until the mouse is unlocked;
  * SetCursorPos discards queued absolute moves but keeps queued relative motion, as Windows does,
    and events older than the warp are kept rather than dropped.
Always: an absolute move is queued after relative motion instead of replacing it.

HB_MAC_MOUSE_THREAD=0 turns the mouse thread off; the rest stays on. Menus and windows that don't
lock the mouse take the old path.

Tested with Deadlock (Source 2, SDL 3, -dx11 on D3DMetal) on engine r22, M4 Pro, macOS 27.2.1,
165 Hz display with variable refresh: camera turns stuttered with vsync and variable refresh,
at frame rates where the same game on Windows turns smoothly, and only while the game held the
mouse. With this patch they are smooth. Not measured with a probe; other games not tried.

